Day 238/250: Largest Magic Square

Problem Statement #1895

Problem Statement Explanation:

A magic square is a k×k matrix filled with integers such that every row sum, every column sum, and both diagonal sums are all equal. 
The integers do not have to be distinct. Every 1×1 grid is trivially a magic square.
Given an m×n integer grid, return the size (i.e., the side length k) of the largest magic square that can be found within the grid.

Solution Approach:

Since the grid dimensions are relatively small (50×50), we can afford a solution that checks various square sizes. However, calculating the sum of every row, column, and diagonal repeatedly is inefficient. To optimize this, we use Prefix Sums.

1. Precomputation
We create four auxiliary 2D arrays to store cumulative sums:

Row Sums (rowSum): rowSum[i][j] stores the sum of elements in row i from index 0 to j-1.
Column Sums (colSum): colSum[i][j] stores the sum of elements in column j from index 0 to i-1.
Main Diagonal Sums (diagSum): Stores cumulative sums along the \ direction.
Anti-Diagonal Sums (antiDiagSum): Stores cumulative sums along the / direction.

2. Sliding Window Search
We iterate through all possible side lengths k, starting from the largest possible value min(m,n) down to 2.
For each k, we slide a k×k window across the entire grid.
For each window, we pick a "target sum" (usually the sum of the first row).
We then verify if all other k−1 rows, k columns, and 2 diagonals match that target sum using our precomputed prefix arrays in O(1) or O(k) time.

3. Early Exit
Because we iterate k from largest to smallest, the first k that satisfies the magic square condition is guaranteed to be the maximum. If no k>1 is found, we return 1.

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    public int largestMagicSquare(int[][] grid) {
        int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
        int[][] rowSum = new int[m][n + 1];
        int[][] colSum = new int[m + 1][n];
        int[][] diagSum = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        int[][] antiDiagSum = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                rowSum[i][j + 1] = rowSum[i][j] + grid[i][j];
                colSum[i + 1][j] = colSum[i][j] + grid[i][j];
                diagSum[i + 1][j + 1] = diagSum[i][j] + grid[i][j];
                antiDiagSum[i + 1][j] = antiDiagSum[i][j + 1] + grid[i][j];
            }
        }

        for (int k = Math.min(m, n); k > 1; k--) {
            for (int i = 0; i <= m - k; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j <= n - k; j++) {
                    if (isMagic(grid, i, j, k, rowSum, colSum, diagSum, antiDiagSum)) {
                        return k;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return 1;
    }

    private boolean isMagic(int[][] grid, int r, int c, int k, 
                            int[][] rs, int[][] cs, int[][] ds, int[][] ads) {
        int target = rs[r][c + k] - rs[r][c];
        
        // Check rows
        for (int i = r + 1; i < r + k; i++) {
            if (rs[i][c + k] - rs[i][c] != target) return false;
        }
        
        // Check columns
        for (int j = c; j < c + k; j++) {
            if (cs[r + k][j] - cs[r][j] != target) return false;
        }
        
        // Check main diagonal
        if (ds[r + k][c + k] - ds[r][c] != target) return false;
        
        // Check anti-diagonal
        if (ads[r + k][c] - ads[r][c + k] != target) return false;
        
        return true;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(m⋅n⋅min(m,n))
Space Complexity: O(m⋅n)



